# The Nodes list's sort row: what order the rows the filter kept are in, under the row that
# decides which rows those are.
#
# Four things to watch, and the fourth is the one that would be a bug nobody could see.
#
#   - Two controls, one shape, two forms. Both rows are a label, a pencil and a value column -
#     the Settings tab's field row - and what differs is only what fits in the column. Three
#     filters are inside INKCELL_FB_SEGMENTED_MAX so all three are on the panel; five orders are outside
#     it, so the sort is the word it is set to. The component measures and decides, which is why
#     the filter above falls back to a word too at the largest glyph scales rather than to three
#     pills with nothing in them.
#   - The action bar names the d-pad per row. On the filter it reads "filter", on this one
#     "sort", and on a node A reads "open" - the keycap whose verb changes is named per row.
#   - The heading's count does not move as the order steps. A filter changes how many rows there
#     are and a sort does not, which is the difference the two rows exist to keep apart.
#   - Under Distance the rows reorder. Under Distance with no fix of our own they cannot, and the
#     value column says so rather than leaving a list that did not move under a word that says it
#     did - the filter row's failure one axis over.

scene demo
scale 4
clock 2026-01-12 19:12
config

pin ECHO

tab nodes
hold 1600

# Onto the sort row. The filter above stays on All, so everything that moves below is the order.
key down
hold 1400

# Default -> Recent. The client's own ranking floats the pinned node and the people you talk to;
# this is the plain question the phone apps open on, and the first row under the lead rows moves.
key right
hold 2200

# Recent -> Name. The answer to the search this client is not going to have: a name you would
# know on sight, at a place you can scroll to, with no keyboard to type it on.
key right
hold 2200

# Name -> Distance. The one a filter genuinely cannot answer - "near me" is an ordering of all of
# them rather than a kind of node - and the reason this row exists at all.
key right
hold 2400

# And one step back, to the order before it. Left is the half this row did not have while A was
# the only press on it: five orders wrapped in one direction meant four presses to undo one.
key left
hold 2000

# The rows under it are still rows. The cursor walks off both controls into them and A opens the
# node the row drew, which is the half that would look right while being wrong.
key down 5
hold 1200
key a
hold 1800
key b
hold 700
